From 63337eb94e95be08b75892d3e94f0492d0493cd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: E-OS <eos@eos>
Date: Mon, 8 Jun 2026 15:20:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] aarch64: route PCIe INTx via ACPI _PRT (removes the
 acpi=off requirement)

On aarch64 under a UEFI/ACPI boot, pcid gets the ECAM from MCFG but no PCIe
interrupt-map (that only exists under device tree), so it could not route legacy
INTx -- nvmed hung waiting for its IRQ. That is why aarch64 needed
`-machine virt,acpi=off` to force a device-tree boot.

Resolve INTx from ACPI instead: read \_SB.PCIx._PRT from acpid's acpi:/symbols,
resolve each entry's PCI interrupt link device (_SB.Lxxx) to its GSI via the
link's _CRS (Extended Interrupt Descriptor), and route it to the corresponding
GIC SPI by opening irq:phandle-0 (phandle 0 = the MADT-registered GIC; aarch64
builds with cfg(dtb) so the phandle IRQ path is available under ACPI too). The
_PRT is read before pcid registers its pci_fd with acpid, to avoid a deadlock
against acpid's AML interpreter build.

aarch64 now boots under pure ACPI (no acpi=off); the device-tree path is
unchanged. Adds amlserde + ron as pcid dependencies.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 8fcf05e6547ced2d4bdf47a30181b0ab554eeb31)
